  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find the exact solution of the inequality $-4(5-x)^{6}+147>-109.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4(5-x)^{6}+147 & >-109 \\\\ -4(5-x)^{6} & >-256 \\\\ (5-x)^{6} & <64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the inequality when we divided both sides by $-4.$\n\nNow, we take the sixth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6]{(5-x)^{6}} & <\\sqrt[6]{64} \\\\ |5-x| & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n-2<5-x<2\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2<5-x & <2 \\\\ -7<-x & <-3 \\\\ 7>x & >3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo make the final result easier to read, we will rewrite it so that the lower number is on the left:\n\n$$\n3<x<7\n$$",
